-Input comes from the selected SDL3 gamepad, the keyboard and the mouse, merged into one Switch controller (`runtime/input.c`). Buttons are ORed; each stick takes the source with the larger deflection. Keys and mouse buttons count only while the game window is in the foreground. `TOTK_INPUT=none` disables both (automated runs). `TOTK_*` settings can also be set in `totk.ini` next to `totk.exe` (`input = none`, `motion = 0`, `rumble = 0`); the environment overrides the file. `TOTK_RUMBLE_STRENGTH` is the rumble strength in percent (0 to 300, default 130; 0 is the same as `rumble = 0`). `TOTK_RUMBLE_CURVE` is the exponent applied to the amplitude before the strength (0.2 to 1.5, default 0.6; lower makes weak pulses stronger, 1 is linear). `TOTK_RUMBLE_TRIGGERS=1` also drives the impulse triggers of an Xbox pad. `TOTK_RUMBLE_HD` (`auto` or `off`, default `off` since three third-party Switch Pro pads broke with it: stuck vibration, slow or held buttons, or a dropped link, #363, #383): with `auto`, on a Switch Pro Controller or Joy-Con the game's amplitude and frequency pairs go to the pad as an HD rumble packet (SDL's `SDL_SendGamepadEffect`, strength and curve applied to the amplitudes) instead of the two-motor `SDL_RumbleGamepad` path; `off` keeps that path. A pad that disconnects after HD packets falls back to the two-motor path for the rest of the session, because some Switch-mode clone pads drop the link at their first HD packets (#363). Other pads are unchanged.
+Input comes from the selected SDL3 gamepad, the keyboard and the mouse, merged into one Switch controller (`runtime/input.c`). Buttons are ORed; each stick takes the source with the larger deflection. Keys and mouse buttons count only while the game window is in the foreground. `TOTK_INPUT=none` disables both (automated runs). `TOTK_*` settings can also be set in `totk.ini` next to `totk.exe` (`input = none`, `motion = 0`, `rumble = 0`); the environment overrides the file. `TOTK_RUMBLE_STRENGTH` is the rumble strength in percent (0 to 300, default 130; 0 is the same as `rumble = 0`). `TOTK_RUMBLE_CURVE` is the exponent applied to the amplitude before the strength (0.2 to 1.5, default 0.6; lower makes weak pulses stronger, 1 is linear). `TOTK_RUMBLE_TRIGGERS=1` also drives the impulse triggers of an Xbox pad. `TOTK_HD_RUMBLE` (default `1`, `hd_rumble` in `totk.ini`): `1` drives the controllers RenHDrum supports (Switch Pro, DualSense with HD Haptics, Xbox One S, Steam Controller 2026, experimental) through `renhdrum.dll` (its own settings are in `renhdrum.ini`, shown in F1 > Controls > HD Rumble); `0` keeps the two-motor rumble. It serves player 1's controller only; player 2's controller, Steam Input virtual pads and Switch 2 controllers over Bluetooth keep their own rumble paths. `TOTK_RUMBLE_HD` (`auto` or `off`, default `off` since three third-party Switch Pro pads broke with it: stuck vibration, slow or held buttons, or a dropped link, #363, #383): with `auto`, on a Switch Pro Controller or Joy-Con the game's amplitude and frequency pairs go to the pad as an HD rumble packet (SDL's `SDL_SendGamepadEffect`, strength and curve applied to the amplitudes) instead of the two-motor `SDL_RumbleGamepad` path; `off` keeps that path. A pad that disconnects after HD packets falls back to the two-motor path for the rest of the session, because some Switch-mode clone pads drop the link at their first HD packets (#363). Other pads are unchanged.
 
